Logistic Regression
🔹 Definition
Logistic Regression is a supervised machine learning algorithm used for classification problems, especially binary classification (Yes/No, 0/1, True/False).
Despite its name, it is used for classification, not regression.
It predicts the probability that a given input belongs to a particular class.
🔹 Why Not Linear Regression?
Linear regression predicts continuous values.
Logistic regression uses a Sigmoid Function to convert predictions into probabilities between 0 and 1.
🔹 Sigmoid (Logistic) Function
σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))
Where:
z = b0 + b1x1 + b2x2 + ... + bnxn
Output range: 0 to 1
If output ≥ 0.5 → Class 1
If output < 0.5 → Class 0
🔹 How It Works (Step-by-Step)
Calculate the linear combination of inputs.
Apply the Sigmoid function.
Get probability value (0–1).
Apply threshold (usually 0.5) to classify.
🔹 Cost Function (Log Loss)
Logistic Regression uses Log Loss instead of MSE.
Cost = - (1/n) Σ [ y log(ŷ) + (1 - y) log(1 - ŷ) ]
Where:
y = Actual value
ŷ = Predicted probability
n = Number of samples
🔹 Types of Logistic Regression
Binary Logistic Regression → 2 classes
Multinomial Logistic Regression → More than 2 classes
Ordinal Logistic Regression → Ordered categories
🔹 Advantages
Simple and efficient
Works well for linearly separable data
Provides probability output
Easy to interpret
🔹 Disadvantages
Assumes linear decision boundary
Not suitable for complex relationships
Sensitive to outliers
🔹 Applications
Spam detection
Disease prediction
Credit risk analysis
Fraud detection
Customer churn prediction
🔹 Conclusion
Logistic Regression is a fundamental classification algorithm that predicts probabilities using the Sigmoid function. It is widely used in real-world binary classification problems due to its simplicity and interpretability.

